gned in 1929 by " [:a {:href "/athletes/61186"} "Johan Anker"] ", a sailor at the " [:a {:href "/results/9225"} "1908"] ", " [:a {:href "/results/9245"} "1912"] ", and " [:a {:href "/results/9351"} "1928 Olympics"] ". The Dragon was superseded in " [:a {:href "/results/10002"} "1976 by the Soling"] ", which became the three-person keelboat class for several Olympics. The event was raced on a 14 nautical mile (26 km) course, which was well offshore."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/63913"} "Thor Thorvaldsen"] ", aboard “Pan”, won the first and fourth races, and had a comfortable lead going into the final race. “Pan” was third in the seventh race, but it was enough to win the gold medal over Sweden’s “Slaghoken”, skippered by " [:a {:href "/athletes/61394"} "Folke Bohlin"] ". Thorvaldsen was crewed by " [:a {:href "/athletes/61242"} "Håkon Barfod"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/62771"} "Sigve Lie"] ", and “Pan” and the same crew would return in " [:a {:href "/results/9564"} "1952"] " and defend their gold medals in Helsinki."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Race #1"] [:th {} "Race #2"] [:th {} "Race #3"] [:th {} "Race #4"] [:th {} "Race #5"] [:th {} "Race #6"] [:th {} "Race #7"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "Pan"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {} "4,746"] [:td {} "1,180 (1)"] [:td {} "879 (2)"] [:td {} "101 (12)"] [:td {} "1,180 (1)"] [:td {} "– (" [:span {:class "label label-primary"} [:abbrev {:title "Disqualified"} "DQ"]] ")"] [:td {} "703 (3)"] [:td {} "703 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "16"} [:a {:href "/athletes/63913"} "Thor Thorvaldsen"] " 